Here is an implementation of a function that finds the median of two sorted arrays in O(n) time and O(1) space complexity:

```python
def median(a, b):
    if len(a) < len(b):
        return median(b, a)
    elif len(a) > len(b):
        return median(a[len(a) // 2], a[len(a) // 2 + 1:])
    else:
        return median(a[len(a) // 2], a[(len(a) // 2) + 1:])
```

This function works by repeatedly calling itself on the two smaller arrays until the size of the smaller array is equal to or greater than the size of the larger array. At that point, the function returns the median of the first and second halves of the two arrays. Since the median is always in the middle of the sorted array, the complexity of this function is O(n) in the worst case, where n is the size of the larger of the two arrays. Since the size of the two sorted arrays is known in advance, the function has O(1) space complexity.</s>

